I reviewed all 20 screenshots you attached (your 7 live gameplay captures and 13 Demon Slayer reference frames) alongside the rendering and VFX code in the project. Here is the honest breakdown in plain words—why the game currently looks like that, and the exact plan to bring it up to true Demon Slayer visual quality.
Part 1: Why It Looks Bad Right Now
Why Rudra looks pure white (or flat red) and enemies look washed out:
The 3D cel-shader stacks three brightness boosts on top of each other: it brightens the texture (1.24x), adds an always-on glow across the whole body, and blasts the character with 4 strong lights at once (total light intensity around 6.8, including an ultra-bright ambient light of 1.85 that erases shadows).
Because cartoon/toon shading snaps bright areas straight up to full white, Rudra's already-pale ash texture blows out into pure flat white (#ffffff) with almost no armor, skin, or cloth detail visible—and hit flashes or rim tints flood him flat red or white.
Why 2D image sprites are still showing up instead of your 3D models:
Vrikodara's blurry grey box (your last screenshot): Even in 3D mode, bosses load their heavy .fbx 3D files on demand when you enter the room. While the 3D boss is still loading, the code falls back to drawing the old 2D sprite sheet—and its semi-transparent grey background box shows right around Vrikodara.
Rudra's shadow clones and dash decoy (your 4th screenshot): They don't use the 3D Rudra model at all—they are hardcoded to draw the old 2D Rudra PNG images tinted yellow/green.
Even your "3D" characters are being turned back into tiny low-res 2D stickers: Instead of drawing 3D characters directly on your screen at full resolution, the engine renders Rudra and enemies into a tiny 192×192 box (and Dundubhi into a 224×224 box), stamps a 2D outline around it, and stretches that tiny picture up to 300px+. That stretching + offscreen edge blending is why Dundubhi and the mobs look blurry/pixelated with jagged light-grey sticker halos around them.
Why Dundubhi's Blood Demon Art looks like messy red confetti (your 6th screenshot):
Dundubhi's Blood Demon Art tries to load 14 flipbook PNG textures from /kaalyuga/vfx/..., but 'kaalyuga' is missing from the server's asset-routing list in next.config.ts (and doesn't exist locally under public/). Every one of those textures fails to load (404), so Three.js renders blank untextured red square planes stacked on top of each other like red paint confetti covering the boss.
Why the slashes, waves, and pillars look "Chhota Bheem level":
Pillars: The pillars are literally drawn with flat 2D Canvas rectangles and thick uniform cartoon lines pasted on top of the painted stone arena—not 3D models—so they clash with the floor and cover Rudra like stickers.
Heavy attacks & slashes: The heavy wave is just 3 stiff curved 3D tubes (which is why it looks like a rigid diagonal beige bar in screenshot 5). The sword trails are smooth curved bands without the sculpted hooked flame tongues, branching thunder bolts, white-hot cores, and pure-black ink edges seen in your Demon Slayer references.
Missing anime impact punch: There is no real Bloom glow, no pure-black Sumi-e ink splash circles behind big hits (like your Tanjiro reference), no 1–2 frame black-and-white manga impact flashes, no glowing molten cracks or flying 3D rocks on slams, and the screen speed-lines are faint grey hairlines (0.32 opacity).
