I just inspected the actual live screenshots of what the game is rendering right now, and you are 100% right—it does not look like Demon Slayer at all.
Here is the honest, simple explanation of why the models and effects look like this right now:
1. Why the 3D Models Don't Look Anime-Painted (Muddy, Dark, or Blocky)
The Mesh Simplifier is Crushing the 3D Models' Normals: When the .fbx models load, simplifyBufferGeometry() crushes their polygons into a 52×52×52 voxel grid to save memory. Anime cel-shading (MeshToonMaterial) and black ink outlines rely completely on smooth surface normals to create clean, flat anime shadow lines across the face, chest, and clothes. Because the simplifier flattens and scrambles those normals, the shading breaks up into dark, muddy, crumpled blotches—making enemies look like pitch-black silhouettes instead of cel-shaded anime characters.
Raw Meshy AI Textures Are Dark & Photorealistic, Not Flat Anime Colors: The textures inside the .fbx files have dark, muddy, AI-baked shadows and grunge built into the image. In Demon Slayer, characters have bright, flat, high-contrast hand-painted color zones (bright skin, vivid cloth, sharp 2-tone shadows) that stay bright even in dark rooms. Right now, Three.js is multiplying dark 3D lighting on top of already-dark AI textures, turning the characters into muddy dark figures.
The Camera Is Zoomed Way Too Far Out (~80px tall characters): On a 1440×900 screen, Rudra is only about 80 pixels tall. At that tiny size, his entire face is 8 pixels across, so you cannot see anime eyes, facial lines, armor trim, or clean ink outlines. In your demon slayer reference folder, the camera is much closer so the fighter and boss fill the screen.
Fake Primitive 3D Shapes Were Used for Decoys & Pillars: When Rudra dashes, the decoy (buildSculptedProxy3D) is literally made of basic Three.js cylinders and spheres (which looks like a glowing green snowman/pawn in Chamber 1), and the pillars are basic 8-sided geometric cylinders instead of detailed sculpted temple pillars.
2. Why the Visual Effects (VFX) Look Like Flat Stickers & Bullseyes Instead of Demon Slayer
Math Circles & Plastic Ribbons Instead of Real Fire / Lightning / Blood Shapes:
Look at Dundubhi's Blood Demon Art on the floor: it is drawing perfect mathematical sin/cos circles (which looks like a flat neon-red target/bullseye on the floor) and smooth, solid-color boomerang strips in the air.
Real Demon Slayer (Ufotable) effects are never smooth geometric bands. Fire Breathing, Thunder Breathing, and Blood Demon Art have jagged, hand-drawn anime silhouettes—roaring flame tongues, branching lightning bolts, chaotic black-and-crimson blood splatters, and scrolling anime flipbook textures mapped along the 3D sword trail.
Old 2D Sprites Are Still Leaking Onto the Screen (The Checkerboard Bug):
In Chamber 1, when Rudra dashes or attacks, KaliyugaArena.tsx is still calling spawnAfterimage() and old 2D canvas effects, which leaves a visible grey-and-white checkerboard rectangle trailing behind Rudra.
The Speed-Lines Look Like a Cheap Triangle Border:
The 36 SVG triangles around the edge of the screen look like flat geometric spikes pasted over the UI instead of subtle, needle-thin anime camera motion lines.
Summary in One Sentence
It looks bad because (1) voxel mesh-simplification + dark AI textures + tiny zoomed-out camera scale ruined the anime character shading, (2) basic math circles and smooth solid-color ribbons were used instead of jagged anime fire/lightning/blood textures, and (3) leftover 2D afterimages/proxies (like the checkerboard trail and green cylinder decoy) are still rendering on screen.
